Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 13, verse 16:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿Or have they attributed to Allah partners who created like His creation, so that the creation seemed alike to them? Say, "Allah is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the Subduer."﴾

In this noble verse, exalted is He, indicates that He alone is the one deserving to be worshipped, because He is the Creator, and none among the creation deserves to be worshipped except the One who created them and brought them forth from non-existence into existence. The purpose of His saying, ﴿Or have they attributed to Allah partners who created like His creation, so that the creation seemed alike to them﴾ [al-Ra'd: 16], is the denial of that, and that He alone is the Creator, as is shown by His saying after it: ﴿Say, "Allah is the Creator of all things"﴾ [al-Ra'd: 16]. That is: the Creator of all things is the one deserving to be worshipped alone. This meaning is clarified in many verses,

such as:

• His saying: ﴿O mankind, worship your Lord, who created you...﴾ [al-Baqarah: 21], to the end of the verse;

• And His saying: ﴿And they have taken, besides Him, gods who create nothing, while they themselves are created﴾ [al-Furqan: 3];

• And His saying: ﴿Do they associate with Him that which creates nothing, while they are themselves created?﴾ [al-A'raf: 191];

• And His saying: ﴿This is the creation of Allah, so show me what those besides Him have created﴾ [Luqman: 11];

and other verses besides these. This is because the created being is in need of its Creator; he is a servant who is nurtured, like you, and it is obligatory upon him to worship the One who created him alone, just as it is obligatory upon you. So the two of you are equal with respect to the obligation of worshipping the Creator alone, having no partner.
